<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Begum Hazrat Mahal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1820–<a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Begum Hazrat Mahal was a wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a>, the last Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. After the British exiled her husband, she proclaimed her young son Birjis Qadr ruler and directed resistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>. When the city fell in 1858 she refused British offers of a pension and withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, where she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/begum-hazrat-mahal.jpg" alt="Begum Hazrat Mahal" width="300" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en.wikipedia.org · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Begum_hazrat_mahal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Begum Hazrat Mahal was named Mohammadi Khanum and was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>, the former capital of Oudh State, around 1820. She was sold by her parents and became a tawaif by profession. Sold to royal agents, she entered the royal harem as a Khawasin and was promoted to a pari.</p><p>She became a junior wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a>, the last Tajdaar-e-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, after being accepted as a royal concubine, and the title Hazrat Mahal was bestowed on her after the birth of their son, Birjis Qadr. In 1856 the British annexed Awadh and Wajid Ali Shah was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. She remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> with her son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> broke out, her supporters, including Raja Hanumant Singh, rebelled against the British. Forces under Raja Jailal Singh seized control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, and she took power as guardian of her young son, whom she declared Wali, or ruler, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. As regent she took a leading part in the rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>.</p><p>One of her chief complaints was that the Company had casually demolished temples and mosques to make way for roads. In a proclamation issued in the final days of the revolt she mocked the British claim to allow freedom of worship, listing the destruction of the temples and mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Mussalmans, the building of churches and the sending of clergymen into the streets to preach the Christian religion. She worked in association with Nana Saheb, and later joined the Maulavi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> in the attack on Shahjahanpur. When British forces recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a>, she was forced to retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, where the Rana prime minister Jung Bahadur at first refused her asylum but later allowed her to stay. She died there in 1879 and was buried in an unmarked grave in the grounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>'s Jama Masjid. After the jubilee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1887 the British government pardoned Birjis Qadr, who was allowed to return home.</p><p>Her role in the rebellion has given her heroine status in the post-colonial history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. On 15 August 1962 she was honoured at the Old Victoria Park in Hazratganj, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, which was renamed and given a marble memorial. In 1984 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> issued a stamp in her honour, and a national scholarship for girls from minority communities bears her name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Begum Hazrat Mahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Indian Rebellion of 1857, from 5 July 1857 to 3 March 1858, <b>Begum Hazrat Mahal</b> proclaimed her son Birjis Qadr Wali of Awadh and ruled as regent; the British reestablished their rule over the next eighteen months, and rebels fought on until 1859.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.78,82.13&amp;year=1858" aria-label="Faizabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6714 1181 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Begum Hazrat Mahal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7164 1451h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7164 1451h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.78,82.13&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
